Subject: engine: the leak and GC dumps are opt-in, not a consequence of NDEBUG

What this does
  ENABLE_DUMPS is defined only when QJS_ENABLE_DUMPS is defined. It used to be
  defined whenever NDEBUG was not, so a Debug build and a Release build disagreed
  about every struct ENABLE_DUMPS touches.

Why we need it
  ENABLE_DUMPS reaches beyond the dump code: it adds `struct list_head link` to
  JSString, which takes sizeof(JSString) from 24 to 40 bytes. JSString's size is
  one of the fields in the AOT layout hash that js_aot_gen_layout computes, and
  the engine refuses a unit whose hash it did not produce. A Release build
  therefore threw away every unit a Debug build had generated, and Debug threw
  away every unit Release had generated. The refusal is a null table, so nothing
  reported it: the program simply ran in the interpreter at six times the
  instruction count.

  A debugging facility must not change the ABI. Deriving one from the build type
  makes the build type part of a layout, which is the whole defect; an explicit
  option keeps the dump code available to whoever wants it without letting it
  perturb a shipped layout.

Hazards
  A build that relied on a Debug build having the leak dumps now has to pass
  -DQJS_ENABLE_DUMPS to get them. Nothing in the test suites depends on the macro
  being set: the dump entry points are debugger conveniences called by hand, and
  the automated tests reach the engine through the JS_* API. tools/aot/check.sh
  gains a permanent check that computes the layout under -DNDEBUG and without it
  and fails if any field differs.

Tests: tools/aot/check.sh (the new layout-independence check), and the kit
outputs for richards, deltablue, nbody and raytrace equal to the interpreter in
both build types.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -198,5 +198,11 @@
 #endif
 
-#ifndef NDEBUG
+/* The leak and GC dumps are a debugging facility, so they are opt-in rather
+   than a consequence of the build type.  ENABLE_DUMPS reaches beyond the dump
+   code: it adds a `link` field to JSString, and JSString's size is part of the
+   AOT layout hash, so deriving it from NDEBUG made a Release build's engine
+   refuse every unit built with a Debug engine -- silently, because the refusal
+   path is a null table.  A debugging facility must not change the ABI. */
+#if defined(QJS_ENABLE_DUMPS) && !defined(ENABLE_DUMPS)
 #define ENABLE_DUMPS
 #endif
